		body: "There is, however, one aspect of future engagement which the enterprise should consider regarding  \nFPIC, and that concerns language. Informed consent requires engagement in a manner and  \nlanguage which the relevant community can understand. In both parties’ material, there is  \ninformation explaining that Tok Pisin language may not be understood in some areas. The  \nenterprise’s material acknowledged the project area involved different cultures and languages. That  \nwould suggest that FPIC would require effective engagement within each of those cultures and  \nlanguages. The importance of engagement in locally understood language was also raised with  \nCEPA, evidenced in the transcript provided to the AusNCP",
