		filter: "Corporate responsibilities for Groups/Human Rights/Environment ",
		subfilter: " Responsibilities related to Workers' rights",
		body: 'The aforementioned lies in the very genesis of the multinational\'s Corporate  \nSocial Responsibility policies, such as the fact of defining workers as partners,  \ndistorting in a certain way their nature and merits, because despite what they  \nmay or may not declare, within a company not all workers are equal. The mere  \nnomenclature with which a worker is called does not, in itself, affect the better  \ntreatment that he/she may receive or the benefits to which he/she may have  \naccess, but on the contrary, it can be used to conceal the asymmetries of  \nresources in labour relations. \nEvidence of this can be found in Starbucks CEO Howard Schultz\'s statement  \nthat "benevolent management should make unions superfluous" and that his  \npartners should believe "in their hearts that management trusts them and  \ntreats them with respect" and "that under my leadership, employees would find  \nthat I would listen to their concerns. If they had faith in me and my  \nmotivations, they wouldn\'t need a union"; and from company spokesperson  \nTara Darrow in her exaltation of the tradition of communicating directly with  \nemployees and not through a union. \nThis does not imply that there is animosity on the part of Starbucks Coffee  \nstaff and Human Resources Management in Chile, who evidently have the  \nintention to improve relations between the union and the company they  \nrepresent, but lack sufficient power to make decisions that could effectively  \ninfluence or be reflected in a change in the multinational company\'s  \norganisational policy at the international level.',
